WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.931
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.931 --> 00:00:02.794
Wednesday the twenty-eighth.

00:00:02.794 --> 00:00:09.112
Calendar's packed in the morning, then it settles into a normal workday with one handoff after.

00:00:09.112 --> 00:00:11.573
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:11.573 --> 00:00:16.828
When the memo's GitHub Action finishes, what happens to the computer it ran on?

00:00:16.828 --> 00:00:18.158
Answer's at the end.

00:00:18.158 --> 00:00:20.353
Weather's nothing to plan around.

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:25.873
Dry, low sixties, a light north breeze, and a few clouds rolling in this afternoon.

00:00:25.873 --> 00:00:28.068
Tomorrow warms up toward seventy.

00:00:28.068 --> 00:00:29.000
Okay, the day.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.392
Calendar has the gym at seven, straight sets again.

00:00:32.392 --> 00:00:39.176
Right after, there's maker space on there from eight to quarter to nine, so that's a quick turnaround.

00:00:39.176 --> 00:00:41.038
Then Godinger, nine to five.

00:00:41.038 --> 00:00:47.623
And there's a lens rental pickup on there at five-thirty, though the exact time still isn't locked.

00:00:47.623 --> 00:00:49.685
After that, the evening's open.

00:00:49.685 --> 00:00:50.616
Alright, news.

00:00:50.616 --> 00:00:53.210
Israel's election came back a deadlock.

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:58.664
Exit polls put Netanyahu's bloc at about fifty-one seats, ten short of a majority.

00:00:58.664 --> 00:01:04.983
The main opposition bloc, led by the former army chief Gadi Eisenkot, landed at about the same.

00:01:04.983 --> 00:01:09.506
Arab parties are projected around eighteen seats, up from last time.

00:01:09.506 --> 00:01:12.233
That's the stalemate the polls predicted.

00:01:12.233 --> 00:01:17.554
Netanyahu still says his side won, and that he'll build a right-wing government.

00:01:17.554 --> 00:01:20.813
Eisenkot told him to accept defeat and step down.

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:25.668
So expect weeks of coalition bargaining, and maybe even another election.

00:01:25.668 --> 00:01:27.863
One for the image side of things.

00:01:27.863 --> 00:01:33.916
With the midterms on Tuesday, ChatGPT is pulling back on realistic pictures of politicians.

00:01:33.916 --> 00:01:42.696
Testers found that if you ask for a photo-style image tied to the election, you now get something illustrated or cartoonish instead.

00:01:42.696 --> 00:01:47.085
It's a small move, but it shows where the lines are getting drawn.

00:01:47.085 --> 00:01:54.269
Real-looking people in real political moments is the thing these companies are most nervous about right now.

00:01:54.269 --> 00:01:56.331
And a nice one for film people.

00:01:56.331 --> 00:02:05.642
Fujifilm says it's putting about nineteen million dollars into its instant film factory, aiming for about twenty percent more Instax output.

00:02:05.642 --> 00:02:10.232
It's a pretty clear sign the company thinks instant film isn't a fad.

00:02:10.232 --> 00:02:11.695
Here's one for Sunday.

00:02:11.695 --> 00:02:21.007
There's a basement gallery on Bogart Street called Incubator Studio Gallery, tucked on the lower level of one of those big studio buildings.

00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:28.190
It opens a group show called Paint and Plaster on Sunday, November first, with a reception from one to five.

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:32.913
It's a four-artist group show, and it runs through the end of November.

00:02:32.913 --> 00:02:34.842
Openings are the easy way in.

00:02:34.842 --> 00:02:41.626
The artists are usually there, the room's full, and nobody minds if you just wander and ask questions.

00:02:41.626 --> 00:02:46.082
It's a short ride from home, so it fits on a slow Sunday afternoon.

00:02:46.082 --> 00:02:48.277
Today's challenge is a photo one.

00:02:48.277 --> 00:02:51.403
Make one picture where the subject is a shadow.

00:02:51.403 --> 00:02:54.795
Not a person with a shadow, just the shadow itself.

00:02:54.795 --> 00:02:59.917
A bike rack on the sidewalk, a railing on a stairwell, a fence across a wall.

00:02:59.917 --> 00:03:05.371
The morning sun is low and sharp right now, so the shapes come out long and clean.

00:03:05.371 --> 00:03:07.300
One frame you like is enough.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.894
Okay, something new today, from making.

00:03:09.894 --> 00:03:12.887
It's about why 3D prints break where they do.

00:03:12.887 --> 00:03:16.146
A printer builds a part one thin layer at a time.

00:03:16.146 --> 00:03:22.997
Inside a single layer, the plastic is laid down as one continuous bead, so it's strong along that line.

00:03:22.997 --> 00:03:27.520
But each new layer only bonds to the one below while it's still hot.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:32.242
That seam between layers is never quite as solid as the plastic itself.

00:03:32.242 --> 00:03:35.568
So a printed part has a grain, a little like wood.

00:03:35.568 --> 00:03:38.095
Pull along the layers and it holds up.

00:03:38.095 --> 00:03:42.019
Pull them apart, and it can snap at a fraction of the load.

00:03:42.019 --> 00:03:48.205
Depending on the plastic and the settings, the weak direction can be half as strong or worse.

00:03:48.205 --> 00:03:53.194
That means the way you lay a part on the bed matters as much as the design.

00:03:53.194 --> 00:03:54.590
Picture a small hook.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:04:00.310
Print it standing up, and the hanging weight pries the layers apart right at the bend.

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:02.372
That's exactly where it cracks.

00:04:02.372 --> 00:04:09.024
Lay it flat on its side, and the layers run around the curve, so the load follows the grain instead.

00:04:09.024 --> 00:04:11.751
A few things help the layers bond better.

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:19.466
Running the nozzle a bit hotter, slowing down, and cutting back on the cooling fan for the parts that need strength.

00:04:19.466 --> 00:04:23.191
Thicker walls usually help more than adding infill, too.

00:04:23.191 --> 00:04:28.977
So before you hit print on anything that'll carry a load, picture how it'll get pulled.

00:04:28.977 --> 00:04:32.636
Then turn it so the layers aren't the thing holding on.

00:04:32.636 --> 00:04:39.619
So: calendar says gym at seven, maker space at eight, Godinger till five, lens pickup around five-thirty.

00:04:39.619 --> 00:04:41.016
And shoot one shadow.

00:04:41.016 --> 00:04:46.470
The answer: it gets thrown away, so nothing survives unless it's saved on purpose.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:48.000
Have a good lift, Ziad.
